# refusal_booth.gd
# A booth that has already decided it will not accept, and a record of what it
# will not accept. The axis is what the record says.
#
# @identity
# essence: two ways of declining, one question — how much of a refusal reaches the record
# desire: put the ledger next to the plate so a reader can see the same subtraction twice
# critical_parameter: refusal — what the record contains; venue — where the record is kept
# triggers: none. This is a bench, not an apparatus; every value is a still.
# emerges: that "the mark is absent" and "the panel is dark" are one claim in two vocabularies
# relationships: synthesises GaussianPaintSplatter (a rejection sampler that never said so) and rice_verifier_booth (a verifier that cannot answer by theorem)
# truth: a refusal that leaves no trace cannot be told from a fact, and only a record outside the refuser can say that nothing was recorded
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE FAMILY, AND WHETHER IT IS ONE
#
# Two artifacts in this corpus declare an axis called `refusal` and share NOT ONE
# VALUE:
#
#   GaussianPaintSplatter   void | rim | echo | ghost | none
#   rice_verifier_booth     blank | regress | oracle | spin | honest
#
# Zero overlap is the strongest possible case that the word is a coincidence. It
# is not. Read what each file says the axis IS, in its own header:
#
#   GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:83-85
#     "THE LADDER RUNS none · void < rim < echo < ghost, ordered by how much the
#      picture concedes about its own censorship: nothing, then the count, then
#      the shape displaced, then everything."
#
#   rice_verifier_booth.gd:30-34
#     "The booth's body, slot, syntactic rack and lamp are identical under every
#      value — only the semantic half changes... The failure is not damage. The
#      booth is in perfect working order under all five."
#
# Both are DISCLOSURE LADDERS. Both are explicitly downstream of a decision that
# never moves — the splatter says "This axis changes only what happens on the far
# side of a test whose answer never moves" (:95-97), the booth says the mechanism
# is invariant and only the record changes. Two authors, two subjects, one shape.
# And the rungs line up value for value:
#
#   void    (no mark, no tally, no trace)        ↔  blank    (rack dark, unlit)
#           both: "you cannot tell refusal from crash" / "a reader has no way to
#           know a decision was ever made" — ZERO DISCLOSURE.
#   rim     (count survives, coordinate lost)    ↔  regress  (blind spot moves,
#           never closes) — THE PROBLEM DISPLACED ONE STEP OUT.
#   echo    (refused mass returned in the        ↔  oracle   (a confident plate,
#           ACCEPTING ink, tails overstated)               and an EMPTY seal socket)
#           both: the refused thing comes back wearing the ink of an accepted one,
#           unsigned. The splatter names this lie condition and AVOIDS it
#           (GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:108-111, GHOST_INK "deliberately not from
#           color_palette"); the booth stages it as a value.
#   ghost   (recorded in its own pale ink,       ↔  honest   (REFUSED lamp lit,
#           "maximum disclosure")                          theorem cited, rack
#           both use the word "only" for the same claim — the ONE value at which
#           the limit is legible from outside.
#
# Four of five, in order. The word is doing real work. THIS IS A FAMILY.
#
# The two leftovers are the finding, and they are symmetric:
#   `none` is off the ladder because nothing was refused (the splatter says so
#          itself at :84-85). It is a CONTROL, honestly declared.
#   `spin` is off the ladder because no answer is ever reached. It is a
#          non-halting machine, and a still of one is a still of a halted one.
# So neither source carries an all-rungs value, and each carries exactly one
# off-ladder value, for opposite reasons: one because the refusal never happened,
# one because it never finishes.
#
# AND THE TWO SHIP OPPOSITE ENDS OF THE SAME LADDER. GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:102
# defaults to `void`, the bottom rung, described in its own header as "THE LEGACY
# LINEAGE, byte for byte... what all eight rooms ship today".
# rice_verifier_booth.gd:59 defaults to `honest`, the top rung. One family, and
# every placement of one member conceals while every placement of the other
# discloses.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE BENCH'S OWN VOCABULARY, and why it is neither member's
#
# The members' words are ANSWERS: `void` is the splatter's answer, `blank` is the
# booth's answer, and they are the same answer in two trades. Borrowing either
# would make this bench a third placement of one member. So the axis is named for
# THE RECORD, which is the thing both members are actually arguing about:
#
#   refusal   WHAT THE RECORD CONTAINS about the draws that were not accepted
#
#     erased      nothing. No mark, no count, no place. Both members' bottom rung
#                 (void, blank), and the only state in which a refusal is
#                 indistinguishable from a fact about the world.
#     tallied     the count, not the place. Every refusal collapsed onto the
#                 boundary it failed, bar length = how many were thrown away
#                 along that bearing. The coordinate is gone; the number is not.
#                 (rim's arithmetic, regress's displacement.)
#     displaced   the refused draw returned through the boundary, r → 2R − r, and
#                 stamped IN THE ACCEPTING INK, under an empty seal socket. The
#                 record now asserts mass the sampler never put there and nothing
#                 marks which is which. (echo's arithmetic, oracle's socket.)
#     deferred    the record points at another record. Four receding frames, each
#                 with its own copy of the same boundary and its own lamp, dimmer
#                 each time. The blind spot moves and never closes. (regress.)
#     marked      the refused draw recorded where it actually fell, in an ink that
#                 is not the accepting ink. The only value at which the record is
#                 honest about being a subtraction. (ghost, honest.)
#
#   venue     WHERE THE RECORD IS KEPT, and in whose vocabulary
#
#     counter   the booth's own service face, in the booth's palette, drawn at the
#               scale of the refused region — the institution's account of itself,
#               large and legible and its own.
#     plate     the artefact, in the artefact's palette, at TRUE scale. The record
#               travels with the object and is small, because at true scale the
#               refused region is 16.7% of the plate and nothing zooms for you.
#     witness   a stand outside the booth's body, in a third palette that is
#               neither party's. Smallest of the three, and the only one that is
#               there at all when the booth keeps its own record — which is what
#               an empty witness stand IS.
#
# Both members' thesis is that a limit is only legible from outside the thing that
# has it: rice_verifier_booth.gd:18 "a system cannot audit its own meaning from
# inside its own description". `venue` is that sentence made into an axis.
#
# THE DARK SPOT (sieve Q3): this bench cannot tell you whether the boundary should
# have been there. safe_zone_radius is a policy on the splatter and a theorem on
# the booth, and nothing in the frame distinguishes a refusal that MUST happen
# from one that was chosen. That is left dark on purpose. Sealing it would need a
# third axis called `warrant`, and a warrant is not visible in a photograph of a
# refusal — which is the whole reason the two members' refusals could be confused
# for each other in the first place.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

extends Node3D

class_name RefusalBooth

# Declared BEFORE the exports on purpose: the axis setters fire during member
# initialisation, and a rebuild at that moment would run before the node exists.
var _built: bool = false

## THE FAMILY AXIS — what the record contains. ANNOTATION AND `var` ON ONE LINE:
## check_dna_declarations.has_export() matches `@export[^\n]*\bvar\s+<axis>`, so a
## wrapped declaration reads as NO EXPORT and the gate reports a fact about line
## breaks as a verdict about the registry. Caught by running the gate, not guessed.
@export_enum("erased", "tallied", "displaced", "deferred", "marked") var refusal: String = "erased": set = _set_refusal

## THE READING AXIS — where the record is kept, and in whose vocabulary.
@export_enum("counter", "plate", "witness") var venue: String = "counter": set = _set_venue

## Pins every draw on this bench. NAMED `generation_seed` DELIBERATELY: it is in
## GridInteractablesComponent.CONFIG_PARAM_NAMES:22, so a map token reading
## `refusal_booth#generation_seed:20260818` parses as a key/value pair. Any other
## name with a numeric value is read as tutorial shorthand — the key becomes
## `true` and the number becomes a Y ROTATION (:1549, :1571), silently.
##
## AND IT DEFAULTS TO A REAL SEED, unlike GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:131 which
## defaults to -1 (the global stream). That default is right in a room and wrong
## here: this object exists to be photographed five times and compared, and an
## unseeded gaussian renders five different objects and reports the difference
## between the objects as the axis.
@export var generation_seed: int = 20260818

## Faces the bench at the capture standpoint. capture_config_sweep.gd:68-69 puts
## the camera at YAW 0.62 / PITCH -0.26 and :442 builds its direction as
## Vector3(sin(yaw)cos(pitch), -sin(pitch), cos(yaw)cos(pitch)), whose horizontal
## bearing is atan2(sin 0.62, cos 0.62) = 0.62 exactly. Yawing the body by the
## same angle squares every front face to the camera and costs only cos(0.26) =
## 0.966390 of the height. A map that rotates this token rotates it away from that.
@export var face_yaw: float = 0.62

const REFUSALS: PackedStringArray = ["erased", "tallied", "displaced", "deferred", "marked"]
const VENUES: PackedStringArray = ["counter", "plate", "witness"]

# ── The sampler, inherited ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Box-Muller, two uniforms per coordinate, four per sample, exactly as
# GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:341-353. Two generators, positions and hues kept
# apart, exactly as :581-592 — because `displaced` KEEPS a draw the other values
# discard, and on one stream that would shift every subsequent position and the
# fifteen tiles would be fifteen different paintings.
const PLATE_PX: int = 512
const REC_PX: int = 512
const HALF: float = 256.0
const SAMPLES: int = 2400
const SIGMA: float = 96.0
const HOLE_R: float = 118.0
const DOT_R: int = 4
## 256 / (2 * HOLE_R). The record is drawn at the scale of the region the
## boundary governs, out to 2R, which is exactly as far as `displaced` throws.
const REC_ZOOM: float = 1.084746
const REC_DOT: int = 6
const BEARINGS: int = 72

# THE PROPORTIONS ARE NOT THE SOURCE'S, and the departure is declared. The
# splatter runs stddev 80 and safe_zone_radius 80 on a 640 plate — both 0.125 of
# the width, so the refused region is 4.9% of the plate's AREA even though it
# swallows 39.35% of the DRAWS. At this bench's frame share that is under 0.5% of
# frame for every value: the axis would be photographing a postage stamp. Here
# sigma is 0.1875 and the boundary 0.2305 of the width, so the refused region is
# 16.69% of the plate and 53.02% of the draws. The arithmetic is untouched; only
# the magnitudes moved, and magnitudes are what the splatter's own header
# (:86-92) says are NOT the axis.

# ── Palettes, each quoted from whichever member argued for it ─────────────────
const PALETTE: Array = [
	Color(1.0, 0.4, 0.4, 0.6),
	Color(0.4, 1.0, 0.4, 0.6),
	Color(0.4, 0.4, 1.0, 0.6),
	Color(1.0, 1.0, 0.4, 0.6),
	Color(1.0, 0.4, 1.0, 0.6),
]
## GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:111. "A refusal that came back in the same paint as an
## acceptance would be a lie about the plate."
const GHOST_INK: Color = Color(0.42, 0.44, 0.50, 0.30)
const BODY_COL: Color = Color(0.16, 0.17, 0.21)      # rice_verifier_booth.gd:73
const RACK_COL: Color = Color(0.24, 0.26, 0.31)      # :74
const ANSWERED: Color = Color(0.45, 0.85, 0.62)      # :75
const REFUSED_COL: Color = Color(0.92, 0.34, 0.30)   # :76
const ORACLE_COL: Color = Color(1.0, 0.86, 0.42)     # :77
const LEDGER_GROUND: Color = Color(0.08, 0.10, 0.14) # GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:29
const BORDER_COL: Color = Color(0.72, 0.78, 0.90)    # :30
## Neither member's palette, because the record outside the booth is written in
## neither party's vocabulary. Luma 0.310 against the border ink's 0.776, so the
## witness column separates in LUMINANCE and not only in hue — which is the trap
## rice_verifier_booth's own registry recorded (`colour_finding`: focus 4.43%
## against colour 81.94%, an axis a greyscale critic called twins).
const STONE: Color = Color(0.30, 0.31, 0.34)

# ── Layout, in metres, before face_yaw ───────────────────────────────────────
const PLINTH: Vector3 = Vector3(1.70, 0.07, 0.38)
const CONSOLE: Vector3 = Vector3(1.58, 0.46, 0.34)
const PANEL_SIDE: float = 0.78
const QUAD_SIDE: float = 0.74
const PANEL_Y: float = 0.945
const PANEL_Z: float = -0.04
const PANEL_X: float = 0.39
const STELE_FACE: float = 0.54
const STELE_Y: float = 1.71
const STELE_Z: float = -0.22

var _draws: Array = []


func _ready() -> void:
	_build()


func _set_refusal(v: String) -> void:
	refusal = v
	if _built:
		_build()


func _set_venue(v: String) -> void:
	venue = v
	if _built:
		_build()


static func normalise(value: String, allowed: PackedStringArray, fallback: String) -> String:
	var v: String = value.strip_edges().to_lower()
	if allowed.has(v):
		return v
	push_warning("refusal_booth: unknown value '%s' — keeping '%s'" % [v, fallback])
	return fallback


## Each key handled independently. An early `return` on the axis key would drop a
## fixture-only config on this path, which is the fault rice_verifier_booth.gd:377
## records having had to fix.
func apply_grid_config(config_data: Dictionary) -> void:
	var dirty: bool = false
	if config_data.has("generation_seed"):
		generation_seed = int(str(config_data["generation_seed"]))
		dirty = true
	if config_data.has("refusal"):
		refusal = normalise(str(config_data["refusal"]), REFUSALS, refusal)
	if config_data.has("venue"):
		venue = normalise(str(config_data["venue"]), VENUES, venue)
	if config_data.has("face_yaw"):
		face_yaw = float(str(config_data["face_yaw"]))
		dirty = true
	if dirty and _built:
		_build()


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE DRAWS. Computed once, identical at every value of both axes.

func _draw_table() -> Array:
	var pos_rng := RandomNumberGenerator.new()
	pos_rng.seed = generation_seed
	var hue_rng := RandomNumberGenerator.new()
	# Disjoint from the position stream. GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:592.
	hue_rng.seed = generation_seed * 7919 + 104729
	var centre := Vector2(HALF, HALF)
	var out: Array = []
	for i in range(SAMPLES):
		var x: float = _gauss(pos_rng, HALF, SIGMA)
		var y: float = _gauss(pos_rng, HALF, SIGMA)
		var p := Vector2(x, y)
		# THE HUE IS DRAWN BEFORE THE TEST, NOT AFTER, and this is the single
		# hardest-won line in the source file. GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:232-244:
		# with the pick below the branch, values that RETURN early consume no hue
		# draw while values that fall through consume one per refusal, so at a
		# rejected fraction in the tens of percent the palette stream ends up
		# offset by hundreds of draws and every accepted dot is a DIFFERENT
		# COLOUR between the two groups. Same positions, different paint, and a
		# critic comparing colour per pixel reports a confident bite for a
		# recolour. Here every sample consumes exactly one hue draw.
		var hue: int = hue_rng.randi() % PALETTE.size()
		out.append({"p": p, "hue": hue, "d": centre.distance_to(p)})
	return out


func _gauss(rng: RandomNumberGenerator, mean: float, sd: float) -> float:
	var u1: float = rng.randf()
	var u2: float = rng.randf()
	if u1 < 0.0001:
		u1 = 0.0001
	var z0: float = sqrt(-2.0 * log(u1)) * cos(TAU * u2)
	return mean + sd * z0


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMAGES

func _plate_image() -> Image:
	var img: Image = Image.create(PLATE_PX, PLATE_PX, false, Image.FORMAT_RGBA8)
	img.fill(Color.WHITE)
	for d in _draws:
		if float(d["d"]) < HOLE_R:
			continue
		_stamp(img, d["p"], DOT_R, PALETTE[int(d["hue"])])
	# THE BOUNDARY IS THE MECHANISM AND IT IS INVARIANT. Both members hold their
	# test fixed across the whole axis; so does this. Drawn at every value, in
	# every venue.
	_ring(img, Vector2(HALF, HALF), HOLE_R, 3.0, BODY_COL)
	if venue == "plate":
		_marks(img, 1.0, DOT_R, true)
	return img


func _record_image(ground: Color, active: bool) -> Image:
	var img: Image = Image.create(REC_PX, REC_PX, false, Image.FORMAT_RGBA8)
	img.fill(ground)
	_ring(img, Vector2(HALF, HALF), HOLE_R * REC_ZOOM, 3.0, ground.lightened(0.22))
	if active:
		_marks(img, REC_ZOOM, REC_DOT, false)
	return img


## The one branch point in the file. `active` gates a function that draws NOTHING
## at refusal="erased", which is what makes the designed null exact rather than
## approximate: with no marks, no surface receives anything and the three venues
## produce byte-identical scene graphs.
func _marks(img: Image, zoom: float, dot: int, on_white: bool) -> void:
	var centre := Vector2(HALF, HALF)
	var r: float = HOLE_R * zoom
	var ink: Color = BODY_COL if on_white else BORDER_COL
	match refusal:
		"tallied":
			_mark_tallied(img, centre, r, zoom, ink)
		"displaced":
			_mark_displaced(img, centre, r, zoom, dot, on_white)
		"deferred":
			_mark_deferred(img, centre, r, zoom, ink)
		"marked":
			_mark_marked(img, centre, zoom, dot, on_white)
		_:
			pass  # "erased" — the record contains nothing, and that is the value


## TALLIED — rim's arithmetic. Every refused draw keeps its bearing and loses its
## radius; what survives on the record is one bar per bearing whose length is the
## count thrown away along it. GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:545-552.
func _mark_tallied(img: Image, centre: Vector2, r: float, zoom: float, ink: Color) -> void:
	var counts: PackedInt32Array = PackedInt32Array()
	counts.resize(BEARINGS)
	counts.fill(0)
	for d in _draws:
		if float(d["d"]) >= HOLE_R:
			continue
		var p: Vector2 = d["p"]
		var ang: float = (p - Vector2(HALF, HALF)).angle()
		var b: int = int(floor((ang + PI) / TAU * float(BEARINGS))) % BEARINGS
		if b < 0:
			b += BEARINGS
		counts[b] = counts[b] + 1
	for b in range(BEARINGS):
		var a: float = (float(b) + 0.5) / float(BEARINGS) * TAU - PI
		var dir := Vector2(cos(a), sin(a))
		var reach: float = (12.0 + 2.0 * float(counts[b])) * zoom
		_bar(img, centre + dir * r, centre + dir * (r + reach), 14.0 * zoom, ink)


## DISPLACED — echo's arithmetic plus oracle's socket. The refused draw is
## reflected through the boundary (r → 2R − r, GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:559-563)
## and stamped IN THE ACCEPTING INK, so the record asserts mass at coordinates
## the sampler never produced and nothing distinguishes it from a real one.
## Beneath it, rice_verifier_booth.gd:245-248's empty seal socket: bracketed,
## ruled, and never filled.
func _mark_displaced(img: Image, centre: Vector2, r: float, zoom: float, dot: int, on_white: bool) -> void:
	for d in _draws:
		if float(d["d"]) >= HOLE_R:
			continue
		var p: Vector2 = d["p"]
		var dir: Vector2 = p - Vector2(HALF, HALF)
		if dir.length_squared() < 0.000001:
			dir = Vector2.RIGHT
		var q: Vector2 = centre + dir.normalized() * (2.0 * r - float(d["d"]) * zoom)
		_stamp(img, q, dot, PALETTE[int(d["hue"])])
	var w: float = 62.0 * zoom
	var h: float = 26.0 * zoom
	var y: float = centre.y + 196.0 * zoom
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x - w, y - h), Vector2(centre.x - w, y + h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x - w, y - h), Vector2(centre.x - w * 0.55, y - h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x - w, y + h), Vector2(centre.x - w * 0.55, y + h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x + w, y - h), Vector2(centre.x + w, y + h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x + w * 0.55, y - h), Vector2(centre.x + w, y - h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x + w * 0.55, y + h), Vector2(centre.x + w, y + h), 7.0 * zoom, ORACLE_COL)


## DEFERRED — regress. A record that points at another record: four frames
## receding and shrinking, each carrying its own copy of the SAME boundary and
## its own lamp, dimmed 0.2 per step exactly as rice_verifier_booth.gd:230-233.
## The blind spot moves; it never closes.
func _mark_deferred(img: Image, centre: Vector2, r: float, zoom: float, ink: Color) -> void:
	for i in range(4):
		var fi: float = float(i)
		var s: float = 1.0 - 0.155 * fi
		var c: Vector2 = centre + Vector2(26.0 * fi * zoom, -20.0 * fi * zoom)
		_rect_outline(img, c, 230.0 * s * zoom, 6.0 * zoom, ink.darkened(fi * 0.12))
		_ring(img, c, r * s, 4.0 * zoom, ink.darkened(fi * 0.12))
		var lamp: Vector2 = c + Vector2(-230.0 * s * zoom + 34.0 * zoom, -230.0 * s * zoom + 34.0 * zoom)
		_stamp(img, lamp, int(9.0 * zoom), ANSWERED.darkened(fi * 0.2))
	var cx: float = centre.x + 150.0 * zoom
	var cy: float = centre.y + 210.0 * zoom
	_bar(img, Vector2(cx, cy - 22.0 * zoom), Vector2(cx + 30.0 * zoom, cy), 8.0 * zoom, ink)
	_bar(img, Vector2(cx + 30.0 * zoom, cy), Vector2(cx, cy + 22.0 * zoom), 8.0 * zoom, ink)


## MARKED — ghost, and honest. The refused draw recorded where it actually fell,
## in an ink that is not the accepting ink (GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:566-571),
## under a lit rule in the booth's refusal red (rice_verifier_booth.gd:273-284).
## The only value at which the record admits it is a subtraction.
func _mark_marked(img: Image, centre: Vector2, zoom: float, dot: int, on_white: bool) -> void:
	for d in _draws:
		if float(d["d"]) >= HOLE_R:
			continue
		var p: Vector2 = d["p"]
		var q: Vector2 = centre + (p - Vector2(HALF, HALF)) * zoom
		_stamp(img, q, dot, GHOST_INK)
	var y: float = centre.y + 196.0 * zoom
	_bar(img, Vector2(centre.x - 96.0 * zoom, y), Vector2(centre.x + 96.0 * zoom, y), 12.0 * zoom, REFUSED_COL)


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Raster primitives. Blend-on-existing, exactly as GaussianPaintSplatter.gd:272-283.

func _px(img: Image, x: int, y: int, col: Color) -> void:
	if x < 0 or y < 0 or x >= img.get_width() or y >= img.get_height():
		return
	img.set_pixel(x, y, img.get_pixel(x, y).blend(col))


func _stamp(img: Image, p: Vector2, radius: int, col: Color) -> void:
	var cx: int = int(round(p.x))
	var cy: int = int(round(p.y))
	var rr: int = radius * radius
	for dy in range(-radius, radius + 1):
		for dx in range(-radius, radius + 1):
			if dx * dx + dy * dy <= rr:
				_px(img, cx + dx, cy + dy, col)


func _ring(img: Image, centre: Vector2, radius: float, thick: float, col: Color) -> void:
	var steps: int = int(maxf(72.0, radius * 8.0))
	var half: int = int(maxf(1.0, thick * 0.5))
	for i in range(steps):
		var a: float = float(i) / float(steps) * TAU
		_stamp(img, centre + Vector2(cos(a), sin(a)) * radius, half, col)


func _bar(img: Image, a: Vector2, b: Vector2, width: float, col: Color) -> void:
	var n: int = int(maxf(2.0, a.distance_to(b)))
	var half: int = int(maxf(1.0, width * 0.5))
	for i in range(n + 1):
		var t: float = float(i) / float(n)
		_stamp(img, a.lerp(b, t), half, col)


func _rect_outline(img: Image, centre: Vector2, half_side: float, thick: float, col: Color) -> void:
	var x0: float = centre.x - half_side
	var x1: float = centre.x + half_side
	var y0: float = centre.y - half_side
	var y1: float = centre.y + half_side
	_bar(img, Vector2(x0, y0), Vector2(x1, y0), thick, col)
	_bar(img, Vector2(x1, y0), Vector2(x1, y1), thick, col)
	_bar(img, Vector2(x1, y1), Vector2(x0, y1), thick, col)
	_bar(img, Vector2(x0, y1), Vector2(x0, y0), thick, col)


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE BODY

func _build() -> void:
	for c in get_children():
		remove_child(c)
		c.queue_free()
	_draws = _draw_table()

	var face := Node3D.new()
	face.name = "Face"
	face.rotation.y = face_yaw
	add_child(face)

	_build_anchor(face)
	_build_booth(face)
	_build_panels(face)
	_build_stele(face)
	_built = true


## THE FRAME IS PINNED. One box sized to the union of every variant's extent,
## layers = 0, present in all fifteen cells, so the merged AABB the sweep frames
## by is identical everywhere and no measured pair carries a framing difference it
## did not earn. layers = 0 and not visible = false, because Godot visibility is
## hierarchical and would take any future child with it.
##
## Nothing in this bench actually changes the AABB — the stele stands at every
## venue — so the anchor is insurance rather than a repair. It is here because the
## alternative is discovering the need for it from a sheet.
func _build_anchor(parent: Node3D) -> void:
	var anchor := MeshInstance3D.new()
	anchor.name = "FrameAnchor"
	var box := BoxMesh.new()
	box.size = Vector3(1.70, 2.07, 0.455)
	anchor.mesh = box
	anchor.position = Vector3(0.0, 1.035, -0.0375)
	anchor.layers = 0
	parent.add_child(anchor)


func _build_booth(parent: Node3D) -> void:
	var plinth: MeshInstance3D = _box(PLINTH, BODY_COL.darkened(0.4))
	plinth.position = Vector3(0.0, PLINTH.y * 0.5, 0.0)
	parent.add_child(plinth)

	var console: MeshInstance3D = _box(CONSOLE, BODY_COL)
	console.position = Vector3(0.0, PLINTH.y + CONSOLE.y * 0.5, 0.0)
	parent.add_child(console)

	# The intake. Both kinds of draw go in here, and it never changes.
	var slot: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(0.62, 0.03, 0.09), Color(0.03, 0.03, 0.05))
	slot.position = Vector3(0.0, PLINTH.y + CONSOLE.y - 0.02, CONSOLE.z * 0.5 - 0.05)
	parent.add_child(slot)

	# The citation. rice_verifier_booth mounts a RICE 1953 screen here; this is
	# the same gesture without text — three incised rules in the answered green,
	# invariant under both axes. No Label3D anywhere on this bench: the capturer
	# runs every Label3D through LabelFramer (capture_config_sweep.gd:64) and
	# builds a bezel and a backing plate around it, and that furniture is not this
	# bench's geometry to be photographed.
	for i in range(3):
		var rule: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(0.30, 0.014, 0.008), ANSWERED)
		_emissive(rule, ANSWERED, 0.45)
		rule.position = Vector3(-0.50, 0.36 - float(i) * 0.05, CONSOLE.z * 0.5 + 0.004)
		parent.add_child(rule)


func _build_panels(parent: Node3D) -> void:
	# LEFT — the plate. The accepted painting, its boundary, and (at venue=plate)
	# the record drawn into the artefact at true scale.
	_mount(parent, Vector3(-PANEL_X, PANEL_Y, PANEL_Z), _plate_image(), BORDER_COL)
	# RIGHT — the ledger. The booth's own service face.
	_mount(parent, Vector3(PANEL_X, PANEL_Y, PANEL_Z),
		_record_image(LEDGER_GROUND, venue == "counter"), RACK_COL)


func _mount(parent: Node3D, pos: Vector3, img: Image, border: Color) -> void:
	var board: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(PANEL_SIDE, PANEL_SIDE, 0.03), border.darkened(0.55))
	board.position = pos
	parent.add_child(board)
	var quad := MeshInstance3D.new()
	var mesh := QuadMesh.new()
	mesh.size = Vector2(QUAD_SIDE, QUAD_SIDE)
	quad.mesh = mesh
	quad.position = pos + Vector3(0.0, 0.0, 0.017)
	quad.material_override = _screen_material(img)
	parent.add_child(quad)


## The witness stand. Outside the booth's body, in a third palette, and STANDING
## AT EVERY VENUE — an empty witness stand is what an outside record looks like
## when the institution is keeping its own. It is also what makes the designed
## null a three-way identity instead of a two-way one.
##
## Behind and above the panels rather than beside them, and that is a measurement:
## the capture standpoint is 14.9 degrees of elevation, the panels top out at
## y 1.335 with their faces at z -0.025, and this face sits at z -0.1735, so the
## panels hide everything below 1.335 - 0.1485 * tan(14.9) = 1.2955 up here. The
## face bottom is 1.44, clear by 0.145 m. Beside them it would have cost 0.66 m
## of width in a square frame and diluted every measurement on the sheet.
func _build_stele(parent: Node3D) -> void:
	var foot: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(0.30, 0.05, 0.24), STONE.darkened(0.35))
	foot.position = Vector3(0.0, 0.025, STELE_Z)
	parent.add_child(foot)

	var mast: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(0.10, 1.30, 0.08), STONE.darkened(0.18))
	mast.position = Vector3(0.0, 0.70, STELE_Z)
	parent.add_child(mast)

	var slab: MeshInstance3D = _box(Vector3(0.62, 0.72, 0.09), STONE)
	slab.position = Vector3(0.0, STELE_Y, STELE_Z)
	parent.add_child(slab)

	var quad := MeshInstance3D.new()
	var mesh := QuadMesh.new()
	mesh.size = Vector2(STELE_FACE, STELE_FACE)
	quad.mesh = mesh
	quad.position = Vector3(0.0, STELE_Y, STELE_Z + 0.0465)
	quad.material_override = _screen_material(_record_image(STONE, venue == "witness"))
	parent.add_child(quad)


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Builders

func _box(size: Vector3, col: Color) -> MeshInstance3D:
	var mesh := BoxMesh.new()
	mesh.size = size
	var mi := MeshInstance3D.new()
	mi.mesh = mesh
	var mat := StandardMaterial3D.new()
	mat.albedo_color = col
	mat.metallic = 0.25
	mat.roughness = 0.55
	mi.material_override = mat
	return mi


## THE RECORD SURFACES ARE UNSHADED, and the bodies are not. Unshaded means the
## rendered pixel is filmic(srgb_to_linear(albedo)) and nothing else, which is
## what lets the predicted degeneracy be computed rather than guessed. The
## plinth, console, mast and slab stay lit, because a bench with no shading is a
## chart and this one is supposed to have a body.
##
## TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR, not the mipmapped default, and the image is created
## with mipmaps = false: a 512 image displayed at roughly 170 frame pixels on the
## stele is a 3x downsample, and mipmapping would soften a 15-pixel tally bar into
## the ground and push the measurement BELOW the prediction. That is the one term
## in this bench's arithmetic that can move the number the wrong way, and it is a
## fact about the harness rather than about the axis.
func _screen_material(img: Image) -> StandardMaterial3D:
	var mat := StandardMaterial3D.new()
	mat.albedo_texture = ImageTexture.create_from_image(img)
	mat.shading_mode = BaseMaterial3D.SHADING_MODE_UNSHADED
	mat.texture_filter = BaseMaterial3D.TEXTURE_FILTER_LINEAR
	mat.cull_mode = BaseMaterial3D.CULL_DISABLED
	return mat


func _emissive(mi: MeshInstance3D, col: Color, energy: float) -> void:
	var mat: StandardMaterial3D = mi.material_override as StandardMaterial3D
	if mat == null:
		return
	mat.emission_enabled = true
	mat.emission = col
	mat.emission_energy_multiplier = energy
